.TH CHTTPSVR_USE 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_use \- append global middleware to the root router
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_use(chttpsvr srv,
                            chttpsvr_middleware_fn fn, void *ctx);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Appends a middleware function to the global middleware chain.  A
global middleware runs before the middleware of each router, and
before the route handler, for every request.

fn gets a next callback.  A call to next continues the chain.  A
middleware that never calls next stops the chain there, and the server
sends the response as it stands.

A global middleware also runs for a request that the server rejects
before a handler can run.  Therefore, a rate limiter, a ban list for each
peer, or an access log that you make as a middleware sees that
traffic.  Three rejections run the chain:
  - A 404, when no route matched the path.
  - A 405, when no route matched the method.
  - A 413, when the declared Content-Length is above max_body_size.
For a 404 or a 405 of a path under a mounted prefix, the chain is the
global chain plus the chain of the sub-router that owns the path; see
chttpsvr_subrouter(3).  For a 404 or a 405 of all other paths, the
chain is the global chain only.  The server decides a 413 after a route
matched.  A 413 therefore runs the full effective chain of that request,
with the sub-router middleware.

The server reaches no handler in these cases.  A chain that runs to
its end therefore sends the rejection response as it stands.  A
middleware that does not call next answers the request itself, exactly
as it does for a matched route.  The request has a method, a path,
headers and a query string to read.  It has no path parameters,
because no pattern captured any, and the server never reads its body.
chttpsvr_req_param therefore returns NULL, and chttpsvr_req_read
returns -1.  The server closes the connection after the response in
both cases.

The send treats a response that a middleware makes for such a request
as an internally generated rejection.  The small unconditional ceiling
on the write duration therefore applies to it.
max_response_write_duration_us in CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT(3) describes
that ceiling.  Keep such a response small, the way a rejection
response is.

Three rejections run no middleware, by design:
  - A 500 comes from an allocation failure of the server.  Application
    code that allocates more memory is the wrong answer to memory
    pressure.
  - A 503 means that the worker pool is full.  More application work
    must therefore not start.
  - The server decides a 501 or a 505 before it routes the request.
    It sends a 501 for a method token outside the seven chttp_method_t
    names, or for a transfer coding that it does not implement.  It
    sends a 505 for an HTTP version whose major number is not 1.  There
    is therefore no routed request for a middleware to see.
The server also sends a rejection with no middleware in two more
cases:
  - Its rejection thread pool is full or shuts down, and the server
    must write the response inline.  This limits the quantity of
    application code that a flood of rejected requests can start.
  - The server cannot allocate the decoded path of the request.  The
    reason is that the server guarantees a readable
    chttpsvr_req_path(3) to each middleware.

This function is thread-safe.  You can call it before the server
starts, and after it starts.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
Each router caps its own middleware chain at 32 entries.  This applies
to the root router, which holds the global middleware, and to each
sub-router.  The library applies this cap at REGISTRATION time.
chttpsvr_use and chttpsvr_router_use return ccol_not_permitted, and
add no entry, on the call that would go above the cap of 32 entries
for that one router.

Dispatch time applies a separate combined cap of 32 for the effective
chain of one request.  That chain is the global middleware plus the
middleware of the router that matched.  Each side of that sum can hold
up to 32 entries on its own.  The combined count can therefore go
above 32 while neither router reaches its own cap at registration
time.  The server then answers the affected request with HTTP 500.

You can register the same path and method more than once.  Each
registration returns ccol_success.  But the server only ever calls the
FIRST handler that you registered, under a policy where the first one
wins.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args when srv is CHTTPSVR_INVALID, when srv is a stale
                  handle that something already destroyed, or when fn
                  is NULL.
ccol_not_enough_memory when an allocation fails.
ccol_not_permitted when the middleware chain of the root router is
already at the cap of 32 entries.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR chttpsvr_register_handler (3),
.BR chttpsvr_router_use (3)
